<Base title="GROUP BY">
  <article>
    <h1>Grouping rows with GROUP BY</h1>
    <p>
      An aggregate such as <code>count(*)</code> or <code>sum(amount)</code> turns many rows into one value.
      Add <code>group by</code> and you get one value <em>per group</em>: one row per payment method, per month,
      per customer. Every column in the <code>select</code> list must either be in the <code>group by</code> or be
      inside an aggregate.
    </p>
    <pre><code>select
    paymentmethod,
    count(*) as n_payments,
    sum(amount) / 100 as total_dollars
from stripe.payments
where status = 'success'
group by paymentmethod
order by total_dollars desc;</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>where</code> filters rows <strong>before</strong> grouping; <code>having</code> filters groups
      <strong>after</strong>. In dbt models you will write this pattern constantly, for example to build one row
      per order from its payments.
    </p>
    <p>This page is static HTML and CSS only: it must ship zero JavaScript.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
